      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 1)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "5627dc6e-c943-439e-ab4a-4531b7f24264"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life and Education</strong><br>\nGrace Hopper was born on December 9, 1906, in New York City. She showed an early interest in engineering, dismantling seven alarm clocks at the age of seven to understand how they worked. She completed her bachelor's degree in mathematics and physics from Vassar College in 1928 and earned a Ph.D. in mathematics from Yale University in 1934. She was one of the few women of her time to achieve such a high level of education in the sciences.</p>\n\n<p><strong>World War II and Naval Service</strong><br>\nWhen World War II began, Grace Hopper wanted to serve her country and joined the U.S. Navy Reserve. Despite being rejected initially for her age and small size, she was eventually commissioned as a lieutenant. Hopper was assigned to the Bureau of Ordnance Computation Project at Harvard University, where she worked on the Mark I computer, one of the earliest electromechanical computers.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Contributions to Computing</strong><br>\nGrace Hopper's work on the Mark I computer contributed significantly to the field of computing. She was one of the first programmers to work on this machine and later on its successors, Mark II and Mark III. During her time with the Mark I, she co-authored three papers on the computer and its applications, helping to lay the groundwork for future computer scientists.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
