      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Invention of the Compiler</strong><br>\nIn the 1950s, Hopper invented the compiler, a program that translates written language into computer code. This invention was revolutionary because it allowed programmers to write in a more natural, human-like language. Her work led to the development of COBOL, one of the first high-level programming languages, which she also co-developed.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Development of COBOL</strong><br>\nCOBOL, which stands for Common Business-Oriented Language, was designed to be a universal programming language that could be used on any computer. Grace Hopper's role in its development was crucial. She believed that computer programs should be written in English rather than in symbolic code, making them more accessible to users. COBOL is still used today, underpinning much of the global financial infrastructure.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Grace Hopper's Philosophy</strong><br>\nGrace Hopper was known for her belief that programs should be written in a language that was close to English rather than machine code or assembly language. She often said, \"We should be able to give the machine instructions like we give them to a bank teller.\" This philosophy drove her to create tools that made programming more approachable for more people.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
