          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Later Career and Lectures</strong><br>\nLater in her career, Grace Hopper became a director of the Navy Programming Languages Group in the Office of Information Systems Planning. She was a senior consultant to Digital Equipment Corporation until her death. Throughout her life, she was a popular lecturer and passionate about teaching the next generation of computer scientists.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Awards and Honors</strong><br>\nGrace Hopper's contributions to computer science were recognized with many awards and honors. She was the first woman to receive the National Medal of Technology in 1991. She was also awarded the Defense Distinguished Service Medal, the highest possible decoration for a non-combat role.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Memory</strong><br>\nGrace Hopper passed away on January 1, 1992, but her legacy lives on. The U.S. Navy named the guided-missile destroyer USS Hopper after her. Additionally, the Grace Hopper Celebration of Women in Computing is held in her honor, inspiring women to follow in her footsteps in the field of computer science. Her work continues to influence technology and military computing to this day.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
